I hit this error three times last week, so let's finally understand it. Here's the code:

fn longest_word(words: &Vec<String>) -> &str {
    let mut best = String::new();
    for w in words {
        if w.len() > best.len() {
            best = w.clone();
        }
    }
    &best
}

The compiler complains that we return a reference to data owned by the current function. That's because `best` is a local String that gets dropped when the function returns, so the reference would dangle.
